WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.905 --> 00:00:16.135
Manny Azizi: Was that enough time, or did I move too fast?


2
00:00:16.705 --> 00:00:17.205
Victoria Wood: I think that's great.


3
00:00:17.205 --> 00:00:18.034
Manny Azizi: I think it's mine.


4
00:00:18.365 --> 00:00:19.375
Manny Azizi: …


5
00:00:19.375 --> 00:00:43.195
Manny Azizi: Great. Thank you guys for joining in. My name's Manny Azizi, I'm from the University of California, Irvine, and this workshop's going to be largely about in-series elasticity, which in and of itself, if you focus just on the tissue, can be a little bit dry and boring, but it'll be really in the context of trying to understand implications for muscle mechanics and whole animal


6
00:00:43.195 --> 00:00:45.305
Manny Azizi: Or Whole Person Energetics.


7
00:00:45.705 --> 00:00:56.875
Manny Azizi: So, that's where we're headed. What is in-series elasticity? So, I'm going to start with some really basic stuff first, just to give context to our trainees, before diving into some more complicated stuff.


8
00:00:57.175 --> 00:01:05.575
Manny Azizi: So, in-series elasticity, is really considered, … do you guys see that, that,


9
00:01:05.695 --> 00:01:09.855
Manny Azizi: the share screen on my screen right now? Should I move it?


10
00:01:10.865 --> 00:01:12.475
Manny Azizi: The little thing that says stop sharing?


11
00:01:13.415 --> 00:01:13.835
Manny Azizi: Nope.


12
00:01:13.835 --> 00:01:15.764
Victoria Wood: No, we just slides.


13
00:01:15.765 --> 00:01:16.405
Manny Azizi: Great.


14
00:01:16.715 --> 00:01:32.534
Manny Azizi: So, in-series elasticity exists in both invertebrates and vertebrates. I consider this really a ubiquitous part of the animal kingdom. I think there's some elasticity and elastic tissues in nearly all organisms.


15
00:01:32.935 --> 00:01:54.115
Manny Azizi: In invertebrates, which I'm not going to focus on much today, you can think of things like the epidem, modified exoskeleton structures as well, like the semilunar process or the exoskeleton itself, are really good elastic structures that are capable of deforming, storing mechanical energy temporarily, and releasing that.


16
00:01:54.615 --> 00:01:55.565
Manny Azizi: …


17
00:01:55.905 --> 00:02:07.084
Manny Azizi: And in fact, some of the most resilient materials come from the invertebrate world, where the resilience of that material can be quite high.


18
00:02:07.165 --> 00:02:21.844
Manny Azizi: In vertebrates, mostly what we talk about when we talk about in-series elasticity are tendons, which we know quite a bit about, and aponeuroses, which I also consider to be sort of tendons, except that they're more like


19
00:02:21.845 --> 00:02:32.475
Manny Azizi: Broad, sheet-like tendons that allow for muscle fibers to attach to a broader insertion site than a long, free tendon, for example.


20
00:02:32.475 --> 00:02:44.645
Manny Azizi: And we've done some work on aponeuroses in the past, showing the complexity of how they're loaded relative to free tendons, but for today, I'm going to sort of focus primarily on tendons.


21
00:02:45.445 --> 00:02:53.334
Manny Azizi: Tendons, as most of you know, are made primarily of collagen, and so when we look at this hierarchical structure of


22
00:02:53.375 --> 00:03:08.345
Manny Azizi: of the tendon. We can see that tropical collagen is one of the primary proteins, and the helices that form are collagen fibers in that 10 to 50 micron range.


23
00:03:08.375 --> 00:03:22.995
Manny Azizi: That then leads into higher levels of organization, like tendon fascicles, which is a level of organization where we've done some work where you can isolate tendon fascicles and pull on them individually.


24
00:03:22.995 --> 00:03:47.914
Manny Azizi: And then you get to the whole tendon, which is made up of many fascicles. Interesting things about tendons is that they're fairly acellular. There are tenocytes and fibroblasts in place that are laying down and repairing collagen, and there's some vasculature, but the general metabolic activity of tendons is pretty low. And that can result in a lot of downstream consequences


25
00:03:47.915 --> 00:03:58.335
Manny Azizi: Particularly when you think about the fact that when you're operating in series with a muscle, the properties of the tendon and the properties of the muscle need to be well-tuned.


26
00:03:58.335 --> 00:04:10.825
Manny Azizi: And if remodeling rates differ significantly between these tissues, you can start to have mismatch as a result of things like, weightlifting or bed rest.


27
00:04:10.825 --> 00:04:19.985
Manny Azizi: Anything that changes muscle properties rapidly, those changes don't really show up in tendons for a long time, and so you can have these periods of mismatch.


28
00:04:20.815 --> 00:04:25.195
Manny Azizi: Now, what we do, frequently in my lab is simply just


29
00:04:25.405 --> 00:04:33.684
Manny Azizi: Grab a piece of tendon, or grab a tendon fascicle, pull on it, and measure the deformation and the force that occurs.


30
00:04:33.685 --> 00:04:51.614
Manny Azizi: Tendons we can think of as nonlinear in their behavior, in that, unlike what would be considered a true spring, or a hooky and spring, they really have two regions. They have this toe region down here, where it's relatively compliant.


31
00:04:51.615 --> 00:05:00.224
Manny Azizi: Leading into a more linear region here, where we can characterize the linear stiffness of that material.


32
00:05:00.615 --> 00:05:08.395
Manny Azizi: This nonlinearity in the toe region really results from the fact that the collagen fibers that are in tendon


33
00:05:08.445 --> 00:05:32.665
Manny Azizi: are sitting in a crimped position at a low length. And so, you can see here, this sort of crimping looks like bad hairdo from the 80s. That really results in some compliance in the very low strain regime of tendons, where that initial pull is actually straightening out those crimps, rather than directly pulling on the material itself.


34
00:05:33.755 --> 00:05:48.234
Manny Azizi: So, we have some nonlinearity that's interesting, and then we can normalize this so that we can compare across sizes or different systems. If you normalize force by cross-sectional area and normalize the length change as a proportional length change.


35
00:05:48.235 --> 00:05:55.425
Manny Azizi: We can then get to something that's much more comparable across systems, which is the elastic modulus rather than the stiffness itself.


36
00:05:56.125 --> 00:06:03.164
Manny Azizi: Now, most often, tendons aren't just stretched, they are cycled through length changes.


37
00:06:03.165 --> 00:06:17.444
Manny Azizi: And so, if we take a tendon and we drive it through some sinusoidal length change, what we notice is that it does return a good portion of that energy back to us, so as we load energy into the system, and then bring that system back.


38
00:06:17.445 --> 00:06:23.575
Manny Azizi: What we see is that we've got this area under that loop that's the energy lost in that cycle.


39
00:06:23.765 --> 00:06:25.185
Manny Azizi: The, …


40
00:06:25.275 --> 00:06:46.585
Manny Azizi: That really defines the resilience of a material, the amount of energy lost as you cycle through it, and for tendons, that happens to be somewhere between 80% on the low end, that's the energy we're getting back, to almost 90% on the high end, which would be the… so we'd be getting back 90% of the energy that we would load into a tendon.


41
00:06:46.895 --> 00:07:10.914
Manny Azizi: However, the fact that it does lose energy with every cycle suggests that this isn't really a spring, that it's really more of a viscoelastic tissue, and that we should really start thinking about it that way. And the work loop, or the hysteresis, is not the only source of that viscoelastic behavior. If we take a tendon, for example, and we just put


42
00:07:10.915 --> 00:07:13.574
Manny Azizi: Pull it, and then hold it at that longer length.


43
00:07:13.575 --> 00:07:17.585
Manny Azizi: What we see is that the force goes up, but it doesn't stay up here.


44
00:07:17.585 --> 00:07:42.524
Manny Azizi: we have this creep and loss in force, which is the stress relaxation behavior of tendons, which is also a characteristic feature of viscoelastic materials. So really, we're thinking about this as, yes, it's a pretty good spring, but it's really a viscoelastic material, and we have MC projects that are ongoing, working with KISA and Miles Valencia, to start to characterize… use system ID


45
00:07:42.525 --> 00:07:47.515
Manny Azizi: to characterize the spring stiffness and the damping coefficient of the material.


46
00:07:47.515 --> 00:07:53.144
Manny Azizi: And to start to think of it more as a material that's viscoelastic.


47
00:07:53.705 --> 00:08:05.924
Manny Azizi: Okay, that's tendon in isolation. I want to move past that, because really what we care about is how are these tissues used in movement, and what now seems like


48
00:08:06.175 --> 00:08:25.785
Manny Azizi: doesn't seem like that long ago to me, but it's been a while. Tom Roberts and I came up with a general framework for understanding the function of tendons, and we really borrowed from a 2000 paper by Michael Dickinson and the Integrative Biology Biomechanics group at Berkeley.


49
00:08:25.785 --> 00:08:42.344
Manny Azizi: That really started to define muscle function as being varied, and really being defined as a behavior that… or behaviors where the muscle acts as a motor and produces mechanical energy, as a strut, where it just produces force.


50
00:08:42.345 --> 00:08:56.534
Manny Azizi: or as a break, where it's actually dissipating energy with every contraction. And so we really thought about tendons in that same way to try to understand, well, what are tendons likely to do in these various muscle function scenarios?


51
00:08:56.785 --> 00:09:09.755
Manny Azizi: And so, we really thought about 3 ways in which tendons can operate during locomotion. First being power conservation, the second being power amplification, and the third being power attenuation.


52
00:09:10.065 --> 00:09:19.704
Manny Azizi: Power conservation is the one that's most well known, and was when we wrote this paper, and so we'll start there, although I won't spend much time on that today.


53
00:09:20.035 --> 00:09:44.964
Manny Azizi: With power conservation, what we're really thinking about are behaviors that are cyclical, where the mechanical energy of the center of mass from the previous stride can be transferred to the subsequent stride. And the way to do that is to cycle that energy through the tendon. And so, if we look at this schematic that I have there, the red indicates where there is a high mechanical


54
00:09:44.965 --> 00:09:45.825
Manny Azizi: energy.


55
00:09:45.825 --> 00:10:09.435
Manny Azizi: And so, what we see in power conservation is that the center of mass has high kinetic and potential energy, it's in the air, it's about to come down, and when the foot hits the ground, for example, and the ankle flexes, the mechanical energy from the center of mass gets loaded into our tendon, while the muscle is simply producing force and operating isometrically.


56
00:10:09.435 --> 00:10:33.935
Manny Azizi: So this would be a scenario where the muscle is operating as a strut, but the tendon is really the one that's cycling the energy from the center of mass with each subsequent stride, allowing us to recycle the energy from a previous cycle into the next cycle. So, in this case, we have energy flowing from the body to the tendon, and then as that tendon recoils.


57
00:10:33.935 --> 00:10:36.584
Manny Azizi: It helps accelerate the body once again.


58
00:10:37.215 --> 00:10:52.944
Manny Azizi: So, in this case, the muscle is really isometric, and so this is where the muscle is behaving like a strut, but much of that strain energy is being cycled directly through the tendon. So, behaviors like walking, running, and hopping are great examples where


59
00:10:52.945 --> 00:11:03.725
Manny Azizi: The tendon is helping us minimize the metabolic cost of locomotion by allowing muscles to minimize the amount of mechanical work that they're doing.


60
00:11:04.945 --> 00:11:18.734
Manny Azizi: Okay, I will say, anytime anybody has a question, you can raise your hand. I'll try to keep an eye on the chat as well, but if Victoria can keep an eye on the chat and transfer questions, I would be greatly appreciative of that.


61
00:11:19.205 --> 00:11:20.525
Manny Azizi: Okay.


62
00:11:20.525 --> 00:11:39.405
Manny Azizi: Next, I want to talk about power amplification, which is one where the body is actually starting from a standstill. So you can think of this as behaviors where we really want to increase the mechanical energy of our center of mass. So let's say we're starting from a standstill and we're accelerating.


63
00:11:39.525 --> 00:11:57.015
Manny Azizi: So, behaviors like jumping, where we're low and we want to get higher, and so that's obviously an increase in mechanical energy. Accelerating or running up a hill, all those are behaviors that require our muscles to impart additional energy onto our center of mass.


64
00:11:57.775 --> 00:12:20.324
Manny Azizi: In these scenarios, we can amplify the mechanical power of our muscles through the utilization of in-series elasticity. And in this case, as we're looking at the energy flow, what we really have is from a standstill, we have the muscle firing. The muscle is producing mechanical energy, which is being transferred directly to the tendon in this case.


65
00:12:20.455 --> 00:12:39.414
Manny Azizi: Now, an important step or requirement here is that we need something to resist the tendency for that ankle or that joint to extend as that muscle is producing force. So these behaviors require something like a latch that, at one point.


66
00:12:39.415 --> 00:12:48.315
Manny Azizi: Resists the forces that the muscle are producing, allowing time for that energy to be stored in the tendon.


67
00:12:48.525 --> 00:12:56.174
Manny Azizi: Once that latch is released, the energy from that tendon is released, allowing the center of mass to get accelerated.


68
00:12:56.225 --> 00:13:11.465
Manny Azizi: Okay? So, behaviors like jumping, accelerating, or inclined running really rely on a combination of muscle work being transferred to the tendon, and then being released onto the center of mass.


69
00:13:11.485 --> 00:13:29.954
Manny Azizi: The key here is muscles are going to be much more power-limited than tendons are, and so the benefit here is really that by having a latch in place, our muscle can contract much more slowly and economically, and generate much higher forces.


70
00:13:29.955 --> 00:13:41.534
Manny Azizi: then would be required to actually directly accelerate this mass. So the muscle can perform at a much slower and more optimal place


71
00:13:41.885 --> 00:14:05.574
Manny Azizi: allowing that energy to be stored in the tendon, which gets released much more rapidly. So where we get the power amplification is that the amount of mechanical work is the same, because it came from the muscle, but the duration over which that mechanical work is released is much shorter in a tendon than it would be for a muscle. So we're really playing with time constants here as we're


72
00:14:05.575 --> 00:14:09.595
Manny Azizi: Increasing the power that's associated with the behavior.


73
00:14:10.605 --> 00:14:21.905
Manny Azizi: So, in this case, what we really have is a flow of mechanical energy going from the muscle, being loaded into the tendon, and then being rapidly released to accelerate the body.


74
00:14:23.285 --> 00:14:39.024
Manny Azizi: Okay, final one I'm going to talk about is power attenuation, which occurs during behaviors where the muscle is aiming to dissipate mechanical energy, things like landing from a jump, so the back half of that jump is you're going to come down and hit the ground.


75
00:14:39.185 --> 00:15:03.305
Manny Azizi: or decelerating, or running downhill. All those are behaviors where the… where the center of mass has high amounts of mechanical energy that we want to get rid of. We want to slow the system down, and our muscles really are good at that, by stretching actively, and really converting mechanical energy into heat and dissipating it as that.


76
00:15:04.155 --> 00:15:15.284
Manny Azizi: So, in power attenuation, what we really get is a flow of mechanical energy that goes from the body, so the mechanical state of the body is quite high to start with.


77
00:15:15.285 --> 00:15:24.434
Manny Azizi: As the person, let's say, lands, that energy goes into the tendon before the tendon actually does work back on the muscle.


78
00:15:24.435 --> 00:15:36.714
Manny Azizi: You may think, okay, well, why can't the muscle do that directly? It can. The benefits of utilizing tendons for power attenuation is that they minimize the direct


79
00:15:36.715 --> 00:15:54.645
Manny Azizi: high velocity or high power input to the muscle, and are much more controlled in the way that the tendon releases energy back onto the muscle. So it's a much more predictable way to load the muscle compared to directly loading it with the environment.


80
00:15:54.645 --> 00:16:08.814
Manny Azizi: So we really, in this case, have the tendon acting as this buffer between the energy that's coming from the environment, or the center of mass, and the muscle that's ultimately having to be stretched in order to dissipate that energy.


81
00:16:10.005 --> 00:16:11.055
Manny Azizi: Okay.


82
00:16:11.175 --> 00:16:23.015
Manny Azizi: Those are going to be the three things I'm going to talk about, and so let me start by talking a bit about power conservation, and I'm going to use some classic work to sort of make this point.


83
00:16:23.795 --> 00:16:33.464
Manny Azizi: In 1992, Russ Bodnette made a really interesting observation, which is that if you track


84
00:16:33.505 --> 00:16:50.785
Manny Azizi: The rate of oxygen uptake, or rate of oxygen consumption in a locomoting animal, and if you have, let's say, a 5 kilogram animal that's running, we know that as the speed increases, the metabolic cost of that behavior increases linearly across most animals.


85
00:16:50.795 --> 00:17:08.094
Manny Azizi: That's simply because as our speed increases, our stance time gets shorter and shorter, we still have to support our body weight with every stride, and as a result, we're either having to utilize more expensive muscle fibers, or have our fibers contract more rapidly, …


86
00:17:08.695 --> 00:17:23.354
Manny Azizi: to accommodate that shorter stance period. That's a well-established pattern that had existed in the literature. What was found with wallaby hopping was that, in fact, once they start hopping.


87
00:17:23.535 --> 00:17:39.395
Manny Azizi: around 3 meters per second, they transition from a weird quadrupedal running gate to a hopping gate. Once they transition to that hopping gate, their gait, or their metabolic expenditure, is largely independent of speed.


88
00:17:39.775 --> 00:17:49.055
Manny Azizi: Okay? And so, regardless of how short that stands time is, it appears that that doesn't bear a significant cost in terms of metabolic expenditure.


89
00:17:50.445 --> 00:18:07.035
Manny Azizi: Following that observation, Russ worked with Andy B. Winter to actually make muscle measurements in wallabies, and here we have force in the top, we've got length of the muscle fascicles in the middle, and EMG activity shown there.


90
00:18:07.235 --> 00:18:12.235
Manny Azizi: What I want you to take away from this is that, yes, during stance, there's this large period of force.


91
00:18:12.345 --> 00:18:16.755
Manny Azizi: However, there is very little length change in that muscle.


92
00:18:17.055 --> 00:18:27.804
Manny Azizi: And so, what was found is that, okay, the muscles really aren't doing much mechanical work at all during the stance phase of locomotion, they're operating isometrically.


93
00:18:27.805 --> 00:18:40.425
Manny Azizi: And we know that the ankle, in this case, is undergoing significant flexion and extension as the animal is hopping along, and so we know that there's length change going on somewhere, it's just not happening in the muscles.


94
00:18:40.555 --> 00:18:54.815
Manny Azizi: So where it's really happening is that that length change is being cycled through the tendon in order to… or during the stance phase where force is being produced, and the muscles are really producing force isometrically.


95
00:18:55.645 --> 00:19:13.964
Manny Azizi: So, what they mapped in that paper was that if you track the amount of elastic energy that's being stored versus the amount of muscle work that's being done, what we see is that as the animal is increasing speed, it is not increasing the amount of mechanical work it's doing.


96
00:19:14.045 --> 00:19:19.295
Manny Azizi: It's increasing the amount of elastic strain energy that's being cycled through the tendon.


97
00:19:19.505 --> 00:19:26.555
Manny Azizi: That is metabolically inexpensive, it's largely free, right? Rather than, …


98
00:19:26.595 --> 00:19:42.944
Manny Azizi: the work that muscles are doing. So, this flat pattern of muscle work across that speed range really helped explain this pattern where… or this independence, the speed independence of metabolic expenditure.


99
00:19:43.865 --> 00:19:55.065
Manny Azizi: So, just to schematize it, what they suggested was as the foot hits the ground, as the ankle flexes, the muscle is producing force isometrically, the tendon is being stretched.


100
00:19:55.605 --> 00:20:03.335
Manny Azizi: In the second half, as the ankle is extending, that tendon is releasing that energy and accelerating the animal forward.


101
00:20:04.105 --> 00:20:05.175
Manny Azizi: Now.


102
00:20:05.365 --> 00:20:16.354
Manny Azizi: This has some important implications for, metabolic expenditure, but it doesn't get quite at the mechanism of what is, what is allowing that savings to occur.


103
00:20:16.555 --> 00:20:21.494
Manny Azizi: There's really two alternate hypotheses, here, one being that


104
00:20:22.115 --> 00:20:28.304
Manny Azizi: Doing mechanical work is simply more expensive than just producing force.


105
00:20:28.535 --> 00:20:33.205
Manny Azizi: That's a work-based metabolic hypothesis.


106
00:20:33.305 --> 00:20:46.344
Manny Azizi: The alternate hypothesis is one that focuses more on force, and the cost that is required to generate the amount of force needed to support your body weight.


107
00:20:47.075 --> 00:20:47.995
Manny Azizi: So…


108
00:20:48.035 --> 00:20:59.305
Manny Azizi: to sort of unravel this pattern, Natalie Holt had this really excellent paper in 2014, where she did some really painstaking work.


109
00:20:59.305 --> 00:21:13.595
Manny Azizi: To not just vary the pattern of length change in the muscle and measure its force, but to also measure the heat that it's producing, which is a direct measure of metabolic expenditure at the muscle level.


110
00:21:13.675 --> 00:21:30.835
Manny Azizi: And so she was really measuring the cost of a muscle contraction when, in A, the muscle is isometric. In B, it's undergoing a stretch-shorten cycle, in C, it's just shortening, and in D, it's really being hit when it's lengthening.


111
00:21:30.965 --> 00:21:33.945
Manny Azizi: So she varied that strain pattern.


112
00:21:34.145 --> 00:21:42.534
Manny Azizi: In order to determine, well, does it actually cost more when a muscle produces mechanical work versus an isometric contraction?


113
00:21:43.435 --> 00:21:56.575
Manny Azizi: What she found was that, no, in fact, it doesn't. When she plots energy for three conditions, the isometric, the stretch-shortened cycle, or the one where it's undergoing shortening.


114
00:21:56.575 --> 00:22:07.715
Manny Azizi: What she showed is that, in fact, the isometric contraction costs just as much. It's largely indistinguishable from a stretch-shorten cycle or even direct shortening.


115
00:22:08.285 --> 00:22:12.845
Manny Azizi: So, the idea that muscles doing work.


116
00:22:12.955 --> 00:22:20.725
Manny Azizi: cost more than muscles producing force, I think doesn't quite hold up. And so this really supports the,


117
00:22:21.075 --> 00:22:44.745
Manny Azizi: the cost of force hypothesis, which simply suggests that, yes, there is a benefit to being isometric, but that's largely because as you start to shorten with some reasonable speed, you are having a reduction in your force capacity to some degree. And that that reduction in force capacity will then require you, if you're trying to match the same level of force


118
00:22:44.745 --> 00:22:50.744
Manny Azizi: Force to start to recruit a larger volume of your muscles in order to meet that force requirement.


119
00:22:51.045 --> 00:23:09.234
Manny Azizi: So, this seems pretty reasonable to me. I'm certainly in the cost of force camp when it comes to the metabolic cost of locomotion, though I realize that deformations associated with sliding filaments likely have some small amount of cost associated with them as well.


120
00:23:10.145 --> 00:23:18.815
Manny Azizi: So, … This… the observations of… Maintaining an isometric behavior,


121
00:23:19.045 --> 00:23:30.994
Manny Azizi: have their benefit regardless of what model of the metabolic cost of locomotion you believe in. If tendons allow muscles to stay isometric, they do bear a benefit.


122
00:23:31.735 --> 00:23:43.074
Manny Azizi: So, during power conservation, we have some metabolic savings that are associated with the recycling of metabolic energy from one cycle to the next. Kisa, go ahead, please.


123
00:23:44.145 --> 00:24:02.315
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Yeah, Manny, so I just had a question about the previous slide and that, paper by, that beautiful paper by Natalie, and, yeah, so, and I guess what I just wanted to say is that, you know, there's a lot of evidence that


124
00:24:02.395 --> 00:24:15.945
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: when you stretch a muscle, like in a SSC, you know, and there's human work and other work on this, before it shortens, like, you can actually see this energy


125
00:24:15.945 --> 00:24:28.565
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: and this is at the muscle level without any tendons, is substantially lower. So, I just wanted to say, I think, like, that this is evidence for energy storage also in the muscles.


126
00:24:28.645 --> 00:24:32.955
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: And, yeah, that was my main point, is that I think…


127
00:24:33.135 --> 00:24:45.875
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: That we've kind of systematically underestimated how much energy, like, the actual muscles are storing, particularly, you know, when tendons aren't super stiff.


128
00:24:46.065 --> 00:24:46.615
Manny Azizi: Yeah.


129
00:24:46.785 --> 00:24:47.655
Manny Azizi: I agree.


130
00:24:48.465 --> 00:24:49.145
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Okay.


131
00:24:52.475 --> 00:24:53.245
Manny Azizi: Okay.


132
00:24:53.305 --> 00:24:58.465
Manny Azizi: … Next, I'm going to focus on power amplification.


133
00:24:58.465 --> 00:25:17.045
Manny Azizi: This is an area where we did a significant amount of work, particularly in collaboration with a large interdisciplinary team. We worked for many years on various aspects of this in lots of different systems. So I'm just going to really sort of skim the very top of this.


134
00:25:17.045 --> 00:25:22.074
Manny Azizi: In 2019, we had a paper, by Mark Ilton,


135
00:25:22.295 --> 00:25:30.315
Manny Azizi: Where we really developed an extremely simple model of the components that are required to,


136
00:25:30.565 --> 00:25:44.495
Manny Azizi: to utilize this kind of power amplification. We came up with four components, a four-component toy model. We have the motor in red, we have the spring in blue, we have a latch, and then we have a projectile.


137
00:25:44.655 --> 00:25:47.625
Manny Azizi: So, what we have is the motor.


138
00:25:48.495 --> 00:25:57.475
Manny Azizi: Producing mechanical energy. In this case, it's going to shorten, and it's going to push our projectile down, and it's going to store energy in this spring.


139
00:25:57.995 --> 00:26:04.915
Manny Azizi: This latch… holds the position of, our loaded system.


140
00:26:06.135 --> 00:26:15.355
Manny Azizi: Once the latch moves out of the way, that allows this spring to then accelerate this projectile and send it into the air, okay?


141
00:26:15.695 --> 00:26:26.304
Manny Azizi: This is exactly the same as a bow and arrow, or a slingshot. This is really utilizing a combination of


142
00:26:26.734 --> 00:26:42.915
Manny Azizi: motors, springs, and projectiles with a latching mechanism in place in order to increase or amplify the output of that system. So you can imagine that in a bow and arrow, for example, your arm is the motor.


143
00:26:43.035 --> 00:27:00.314
Manny Azizi: The bow itself is the spring. You're loading energy into that spring. Your fingers holding the arrow are really the latch. When they let go, you've unlatched the system, and that arrow is gonna fly, and it's gonna fly much further than you can throw it.


144
00:27:00.505 --> 00:27:02.234
Manny Azizi: Right? So…


145
00:27:02.495 --> 00:27:19.464
Manny Azizi: This increases the output of the, let's say, the power output. You can think of it as kinetic energy at takeoff, the height that that projectile goes to, it doesn't really matter what performance parameter it is. It's going to generally do better than the muscle.


146
00:27:20.365 --> 00:27:40.535
Manny Azizi: So, we sort of thought about it this way. During the loading phase, we're loading potential energy into our spring. It's going to be latched, and so we're going to have that energy sort of stay up here. There's going to be an unlatching phase, which is the rate at which that yellow box gets out of the way.


147
00:27:41.125 --> 00:27:49.795
Manny Azizi: And then once it gets out of the way, we see that there's some rapid extension of that spring that then sends our projectile up into the air.


148
00:27:50.215 --> 00:27:54.084
Manny Azizi: What's key in these systems are really a couple of things.


149
00:27:54.215 --> 00:28:12.545
Manny Azizi: One is how good that latch is, okay? And we played around with making that box a rounded edge, lots of very… and changing the speed at which you pull it back, and so on. The shorter that unlatching phase is, the higher the performance of the system.


150
00:28:12.965 --> 00:28:23.124
Manny Azizi: Anytime that slows down, you're actually losing energy to the latch itself, because during the unlatching, you're starting to lose some of the energy here.


151
00:28:23.255 --> 00:28:34.124
Manny Azizi: Okay, so the longer it takes, the poorer the latch, the more of that energy gets sapped from the system before you're actually applying it to the projectile that you're accelerating.


152
00:28:34.935 --> 00:28:35.865
Manny Azizi: …


153
00:28:35.985 --> 00:28:45.725
Manny Azizi: So that's one thing. Latching's extremely important, and it's actually really regulating the flow of mechanical energy, so it's really the critical piece in this puzzle.


154
00:28:45.795 --> 00:29:03.214
Manny Azizi: The second is, if you start to play around with the material… the mechanical properties of the motor and the spring, their tuned relationship is also critical. That, in fact, if those are not really working particularly well together, you're simply not going to have the system perform as well.


155
00:29:03.935 --> 00:29:08.724
Manny Azizi: So, taking this model, we really thought, okay, well, we gotta place,


156
00:29:08.855 --> 00:29:25.505
Manny Azizi: we have to replace that generic motor with behaviors that are more muscle-like. And so we know that the force-length properties of skeletal muscle really restrict deformation, or limit the amount of length changes that are available to the system.


157
00:29:27.925 --> 00:29:44.184
Manny Azizi: when they do, that's likely to influence the properties of the spring that make sense. So in this case, what we… what we were able to show was that, in fact, if you start from some long length, but your spring is really compliant.


158
00:29:45.285 --> 00:29:54.854
Manny Azizi: rather than load a lot of energy into that system. And so, what I'm doing here is I'm starting a spring, and then I'm ending it here, right? So…


159
00:29:54.875 --> 00:30:07.124
Manny Azizi: As the muscle contracts against the spring, the spring's very compliant, the muscle shortens a lot quickly, but because there isn't a lot of force associated with that spring, there isn't a lot of energy being stored in it.


160
00:30:07.125 --> 00:30:16.945
Manny Azizi: So when it's too compliant, we get to lengths where muscles aren't producing great force before we've been able to load a lot of energy into the system.


161
00:30:17.185 --> 00:30:18.685
Manny Azizi: Okay.


162
00:30:20.555 --> 00:30:25.905
Manny Azizi: If we're too stiff, the…


163
00:30:26.395 --> 00:30:33.295
Manny Azizi: There's very little shortening that's occurring in the tendon, so that… so the muscle effectively is…


164
00:30:33.615 --> 00:30:41.804
Manny Azizi: More isometric with little shortening of the tendon itself, and so the area under that curve is also a little bit small.


165
00:30:42.545 --> 00:30:50.475
Manny Azizi: Those are both, cases where we're able to calculate what actually the optimal is, which is right in between those.


166
00:30:50.675 --> 00:31:02.795
Manny Azizi: Where the optimal is not too stiff, not too compliant, allows a significant amount of shortening, but doesn't shorten so much to where the muscle's forces start to really drop off.


167
00:31:03.175 --> 00:31:21.554
Manny Azizi: And so the biggest area we get, the area under that curve is really the energy that's stored in the tendon, and so the size of that triangle should be interpreted as how much energy I was able to put into that tendon. So this matching between tendon and muscle properties really starts to be critical.


168
00:31:23.265 --> 00:31:36.544
Manny Azizi: We thought we could test this in a system that I'd worked on for a while, which are jumping frogs. And in this jumping frog, we're going to see it jump off this force plate. In this case.


169
00:31:36.615 --> 00:31:50.184
Manny Azizi: The tendons are the springs, the hindlimb muscles are the motors, the latch in this case, is actually the mechanical advantage of the limb itself, which changes dynamically throughout the jump.


170
00:31:50.185 --> 00:32:04.804
Manny Azizi: So when the animal is in this posture where the limb is highly flexed, the mechanical advantage is quite low, and as the muscle produces force, that low mechanical advantage, combined with the inertia of the body itself.


171
00:32:04.925 --> 00:32:13.214
Manny Azizi: Hold the animal long enough for it to store energy in its tendons before it starts to extend its limbs


172
00:32:13.465 --> 00:32:20.705
Manny Azizi: Creating a positive feedback loop where the increase in its mechanical advantage starts to release the energy in the tendon.


173
00:32:21.845 --> 00:32:25.114
Manny Azizi: Here's the animal taking off, and you can see that it's


174
00:32:25.675 --> 00:32:29.365
Manny Azizi: Quite an excellent jumper, accelerating nicely out of our view.


175
00:32:31.205 --> 00:32:48.255
Manny Azizi: We thought the idea of matching muscle and tendon properties could potentially help us with a conundrum that we had when we compared three species of frogs and showed that their behavior during jumps are significantly different.


176
00:32:48.455 --> 00:33:00.405
Manny Azizi: So these are, power in watts per kilogram of muscle mass plotted against time for a jumping tree frog, a bullfrog, and a toad.


177
00:33:00.695 --> 00:33:09.455
Manny Azizi: So we see that the tree frog generates significantly higher power outputs during a jump compared to the bullfrog, and the toad comes in dead last.


178
00:33:10.635 --> 00:33:14.545
Manny Azizi: Now, our original foray was to try to explain


179
00:33:14.785 --> 00:33:21.245
Manny Azizi: this difference through the capacity of their muscles. This was in 2011.


180
00:33:21.505 --> 00:33:26.125
Manny Azizi: But it turns out that despite the fact that the jump power


181
00:33:26.435 --> 00:33:36.134
Manny Azizi: increases substantially across these three species. Their muscle power, shown in white, is largely constant across all of those.


182
00:33:36.495 --> 00:33:48.824
Manny Azizi: So, differences in mechanical power capacity of skeletal muscles, we're not really able to explain, the differences that we observe across these three species and their jump performance.


183
00:33:49.735 --> 00:34:00.924
Manny Azizi: What we were able to show, once we started to really dig into the mechanical properties of the tendons and muscles, is that what we found was that


184
00:34:01.465 --> 00:34:18.904
Manny Azizi: If you track the behavior of the muscle and the tendon, and so in this case, I'm showing you a sample where the muscle's starting here, shortening to some, to some length, producing force, and the area gives us the capacity for that muscle to store mechanical energy.


185
00:34:19.165 --> 00:34:20.085
Manny Azizi: Go ahead.


186
00:34:20.685 --> 00:34:22.565
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Finish your slide, and then I'll chime in.


187
00:34:22.565 --> 00:34:23.255
Manny Azizi: Okay.


188
00:34:23.655 --> 00:34:32.775
Manny Azizi: So, what we see is that the area, defines the amount of mechanical energy that each of those muscles can store in their tendons.


189
00:34:32.985 --> 00:34:42.664
Manny Azizi: What we see is that a couple of things differ when it comes to the tree frogs potentially explaining their high performance. One.


190
00:34:42.895 --> 00:34:51.904
Manny Azizi: The specific force, or their stress capacity, is quite a bit higher, particularly if you normalize it to muscle mass.


191
00:34:52.115 --> 00:35:03.745
Manny Azizi: They have highly pinnate muscles that are capable of higher forces per muscle mass, and are able to get to these higher forces than the other species.


192
00:35:04.395 --> 00:35:23.844
Manny Azizi: Matching that high force capacity is the fact that their tendons are also stiffer. And the way you can interpret that is, what does the slope look like in that line, when the muscle is shortening against the stretch of tendon? Really, the slope of these lines is also the stiffness of the tendon.


193
00:35:23.955 --> 00:35:32.965
Manny Azizi: So here, what we see is that tree frogs have evolved higher force capacity through changes in the structural properties of their muscles.


194
00:35:32.965 --> 00:35:45.015
Manny Azizi: And are operating with higher, with stiffer tendons in order to increase the amount of mechanical energy that they can store, in their tendons during a jump.


195
00:35:45.365 --> 00:35:58.685
Manny Azizi: So this mismatch, this idea that the tendons and muscles have to match really does provide some level of explanation for the variation in jumping performance that we see across three species.


196
00:35:59.245 --> 00:36:00.335
Manny Azizi: Kisa?


197
00:36:01.585 --> 00:36:12.685
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Yeah, so I guess my question is, how did you estimate the peak power of the muscles, and, you know, was that, like, the isotonic peak power?


198
00:36:12.685 --> 00:36:13.585
Manny Azizi: It is.


199
00:36:13.585 --> 00:36:22.824
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Yeah, yeah. So, like, my little chirpy, you know, sort of, comment would be.


200
00:36:23.275 --> 00:36:39.914
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: So, you know, that might or might not differ, you know, if we did, like, some… if we can… if our group can think of a measurement of how we might estimate the dynamic muscle power when you actually have changing load.


201
00:36:40.215 --> 00:36:48.274
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: It might be the same still across the different species, but it might just be that the isotonic


202
00:36:48.605 --> 00:36:55.575
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Constrain is… is maybe systematically underestimating what the muscle power actually is.


203
00:36:56.365 --> 00:37:01.285
Manny Azizi: Well, it has to be sustained, though, because this is a behavior that takes


204
00:37:01.665 --> 00:37:06.295
Manny Azizi: Let's say 300 milliseconds of loading, and so…


205
00:37:06.685 --> 00:37:17.674
Manny Azizi: if you think about the muscle power requirements, they can't be transients. They can't be transient measures of muscle power, they have to be sustained measures of muscle power.


206
00:37:17.825 --> 00:37:27.605
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Right, I would just say, like, the… that the, … you know, I was thinking of, like, if you look, for example, at the recent paper, you know, from, …


207
00:37:27.745 --> 00:37:31.554
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Peterson and Roberts.


208
00:37:31.715 --> 00:37:41.244
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: you know, that there is some elastic recoil of the muscles as well, and so, so I think…


209
00:37:41.465 --> 00:37:44.335
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: that… ….


210
00:37:44.335 --> 00:37:44.935
Manny Azizi: with the election.


211
00:37:44.935 --> 00:37:46.625
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: For a given, for….


212
00:37:46.625 --> 00:37:48.314
Manny Azizi: in the wrong direction.


213
00:37:48.355 --> 00:37:53.304
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Well, I think for a given… I think for what…


214
00:37:53.665 --> 00:38:12.605
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: My interpretation is, is that, and we can talk about more about this offline, but for a given velocity, muscles can actually produce quite a bit more force when their load is reducing than they can when the load is constant.


215
00:38:14.205 --> 00:38:15.125
Manny Azizi: I agree.


216
00:38:15.555 --> 00:38:17.034
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Yes. Yeah.


217
00:38:20.035 --> 00:38:20.765
Manny Azizi: Okay.


218
00:38:21.325 --> 00:38:28.065
Manny Azizi: The other, the other bit of information that we were able to glean from, that


219
00:38:28.645 --> 00:38:32.645
Manny Azizi: That little, toy model that we came up with.


220
00:38:33.135 --> 00:38:41.185
Manny Azizi: Was that, in fact, there's a really interesting scaling relationship between when these tendon-driven behaviors,


221
00:38:41.315 --> 00:38:52.414
Manny Azizi: Make sense, and are useful, and are beneficial in terms of performance, and when really just directly actuating the system with muscles is more beneficial.


222
00:38:52.415 --> 00:39:11.455
Manny Azizi: And the way this was derived was for a very specific set of scenarios where we gave some muscle-like properties to our motor, and we ran sort of two different spring models, one where it's an ideal spring and one where it's got some mass and some realistic properties.


223
00:39:12.125 --> 00:39:28.234
Manny Azizi: What we found was if you measure, let's say, maximum power, we also did this for maximum kinetic energy of the projectile, maximum momentum, so we had lots of different metrics that we looked at. The pattern was quite consistent, although the mass


224
00:39:28.385 --> 00:39:36.464
Manny Azizi: It was a little different based on what metric you use, not significantly, though, on a large scale. What we found was that if you are small.


225
00:39:37.485 --> 00:39:39.735
Manny Azizi: Springs make a ton of sense.


226
00:39:40.055 --> 00:39:53.484
Manny Azizi: And that your power outputs being spring-driven significantly outperform muscle-driven behavior, where you're directly actuating the system with skeletal muscles.


227
00:39:53.895 --> 00:39:58.325
Manny Azizi: And then you get to some transitional length, or size.


228
00:39:58.435 --> 00:40:13.485
Manny Azizi: Where beyond that point, you start to really lose the benefit of rapid delivery of mechanical power from a tendon that exceeds the rate at which power can be produced by the skeletal muscle.


229
00:40:14.255 --> 00:40:20.304
Manny Azizi: That happens to be on the order of, let's say, 5 to 10 gram masses.


230
00:40:20.345 --> 00:40:34.655
Manny Azizi: And the way that we… and the reasoning behind that is that the… as you get bigger, that… the mass is going to have inertia associated with it. It's going to take time to accelerate that mass.


231
00:40:35.055 --> 00:40:53.444
Manny Azizi: That time effectively saps that power that the tendon is producing. So it doesn't matter how fast the tendon is recoiling, if the mass is so high that its inertia is going to take some amount of time to get to its high… to its peak velocity anyway.


232
00:40:53.695 --> 00:41:05.995
Manny Azizi: So, in this case, the higher, higher body masses tend to have this higher inertial, constraint, which then limits their utility, the utility of spring-driven systems.


233
00:41:07.175 --> 00:41:08.855
Manny Azizi: What was interesting was.


234
00:41:08.885 --> 00:41:25.994
Manny Azizi: I was on this team, my system was frogs, and so we were really right in that transitional zone where, for some species, it made a lot of sense to be spring-driven, and for some species, it made a lot of sense to be… to be directly muscle-driven.


235
00:41:25.995 --> 00:41:32.155
Manny Azizi: The way we started to think about this was that, well, maybe these things, at some point, …


236
00:41:32.375 --> 00:41:34.764
Manny Azizi: These things may not be mutually exclusive.


237
00:41:34.825 --> 00:41:53.545
Manny Azizi: That you may not simply be a spring-driven system or a muscle-driven system, but that those things may be able to work in concert once the system gets slow enough for the muscle to be able to generate the powers needed to continue to contribute to the, to the takeoff phase.


238
00:41:53.665 --> 00:42:05.454
Manny Azizi: And so, we came up with this prediction that, well, I think frogs are in this nice little range where they can utilize both, simultaneously utilize both spring and muscle actuation.


239
00:42:05.715 --> 00:42:07.774
Manny Azizi: During their takeoff phase.


240
00:42:08.185 --> 00:42:16.955
Manny Azizi: The way we tested this prediction, was through, what we call virtual reality muscle experiments.


241
00:42:17.135 --> 00:42:36.774
Manny Azizi: In these experiments, what we effectively have is a… an isolated muscle tendon unit, where we're independently measuring the length changes of the muscle fascicles. In turn, that gives us the length changes of the tendon. We're also stimulating this muscle artificially.


242
00:42:37.245 --> 00:42:51.394
Manny Azizi: However, rather than just measuring the force output of various contractions, what we have is a real-time feedback controller that's in place that allows us to take the force output of our muscle in every time step.


243
00:42:51.415 --> 00:43:02.294
Manny Azizi: Feed it into a, an in-silico model of a jointed system where we've got a mass up on top.


244
00:43:02.535 --> 00:43:09.524
Manny Azizi: This is really not there. That's actually the muscle tendon unit in real life that's, that's producing the forces.


245
00:43:09.525 --> 00:43:24.805
Manny Azizi: And it's actuating it through this jointed system that's got some leverage that we matched to our frogs. It's got a mass that we match to our frogs. And so, we're really doing these sort of hybrid experiments where you have real biological tissues.


246
00:43:24.805 --> 00:43:27.865
Manny Azizi: Interacting in real time with a model.


247
00:43:28.085 --> 00:43:38.534
Manny Azizi: Okay, so this is a vertical jumping model. It's a pretty simple thing that we could… that we could code up, and have the muscle interact with it in real time.


248
00:43:39.505 --> 00:43:55.085
Manny Azizi: And then, in order to really test the relative contributions of muscles and springs in this system, we did this one additional manipulation, which is that we altered the temperature of our system.


249
00:43:55.255 --> 00:43:59.924
Manny Azizi: That allows us to alter our muscle properties significantly.


250
00:44:00.075 --> 00:44:16.824
Manny Azizi: while retaining our tendon properties. So tendons are not really temperature sensitive, they're not undergoing enzymatic reactions, they're a mechanical tissue, whereas muscles are undergoing enzymatic reactions, and so as a result, they're going to be much more temperature sensitive.


251
00:44:17.375 --> 00:44:22.524
Manny Azizi: So, we ran this model and these experiments, and so, …


252
00:44:22.905 --> 00:44:38.134
Manny Azizi: Same with the frog system. The mechanical advantage of that jointed model is increasing through time. It's storing energy, it's releasing energy, it's behaving in ways that we think resemble our frogs pretty well. So here we've got


253
00:44:38.185 --> 00:44:50.085
Manny Azizi: The joint angle of the model at 10 and 20 degrees, those sort of get to the same place, they all take off, regardless of the temperature, it just takes a while longer for the 10 degree to do that.


254
00:44:50.525 --> 00:45:07.895
Manny Azizi: Here we have muscle force. We have higher forces in red. That's not really a thermal effect, that's more of an effect of the rate of force development increasing significantly with, with our, with our increase in temperature.


255
00:45:08.565 --> 00:45:20.804
Manny Azizi: And then we've got the, our fascicle lengths, that's our muscle shortening during this behavior in order to store energy into the tendon and then release it when the force starts to come down.


256
00:45:21.195 --> 00:45:35.364
Manny Azizi: The part of this trace that we were really interested in were these parts down here, where you have significant amount of shortening, really near the end, like, really close to the takeoff of this muscle… of this model.


257
00:45:36.725 --> 00:45:52.334
Manny Azizi: Here's what we were able to find. So, I have force length curves plotted here. I've got two conditions, cold in blue, hot in red. We have our muscle, shortening.


258
00:45:52.605 --> 00:46:02.535
Manny Azizi: That dashed line is kind of the inertial constraint of our model. We have to get to that force before the model starts to accelerate.


259
00:46:03.125 --> 00:46:20.885
Manny Azizi: What we see is that when it's hot, muscle develops force, shortens to stretch the tendon, but it overshoots that threshold, because its rate of force development's pretty high. Stores a good amount of energy in that first phase, which is


260
00:46:20.985 --> 00:46:30.214
Manny Azizi: pre-threshreshold, then it overshoots the threshold in Phase 2, and then it continues to shorten


261
00:46:30.345 --> 00:46:38.604
Manny Azizi: during Phase 3, which is when the tendon is also recoiling. So, in the first part, we have the muscle loading the tendon.


262
00:46:38.885 --> 00:46:51.035
Manny Azizi: Actually, in the first two parts, we have the muscle loading the tendon, and in phase three, the tendon is unloading, but the muscle is also continuing to shorten rapidly and produce high power outputs.


263
00:46:51.825 --> 00:47:08.565
Manny Azizi: So, what we really have is evidence of a system where the muscle is storing energy into the tendon, the tendon begins to recoil, but the muscle is not done and is not getting out of the way. It can continue to impart mechanical energy into the system during the takeoff phase.


264
00:47:08.955 --> 00:47:09.725
Manny Azizi: Okay?


265
00:47:10.375 --> 00:47:22.315
Manny Azizi: So, what we see when we compare the two temperatures is that as you slow the rate of force development down, we don't overshoot our threshold by much when the muscle gets cold.


266
00:47:22.765 --> 00:47:42.425
Manny Azizi: But interestingly, where we see the biggest difference is in this third phase, where the muscle is imparting additional power onto the system, we see that when we decrease the temperature, and we decrease the capacity of the muscle to generate mechanical power, that that's the part where we start to lose.


267
00:47:43.125 --> 00:47:57.904
Manny Azizi: Now, what this helped explain was something interesting, which was that it's been long held that elastically driven behaviors are not really going to be temperature sensitive.


268
00:47:58.125 --> 00:48:02.714
Manny Azizi: But also, that frog jumping is highly temperature sensitive.


269
00:48:02.715 --> 00:48:22.174
Manny Azizi: And so that mismatch in ideas is really explained by the fact that this isn't a purely elastic behavior driven by springs, but it's that it's really a combination. And that the part you start to lose when the temperature gets cold is that additional muscle power during the unloading phase of the behavior.


270
00:48:23.315 --> 00:48:34.794
Manny Azizi: I can show that more quantitatively here. What we see is that the amount of energy stored slowly during that early phase is the same across temperature.


271
00:48:35.605 --> 00:48:41.265
Manny Azizi: The overshoot helps us load a little bit more energy when things get hotter.


272
00:48:42.585 --> 00:48:44.164
Manny Azizi: But interestingly.


273
00:48:44.885 --> 00:48:56.384
Manny Azizi: The power in the… the power of the recoil of the tendon and the power of the muscle during unloading is where we see the highest


274
00:48:56.665 --> 00:48:58.265
Manny Azizi: temperature effects.


275
00:48:58.415 --> 00:49:15.464
Manny Azizi: And in fact, what we see is that there's almost a 50-50 split between the amount of energy that's coming out of the tendon and the amount of additional energy that's being put back into the system by the muscle during the unloading phase of the behavior.


276
00:49:15.545 --> 00:49:16.935
Manny Azizi: So, really.


277
00:49:16.945 --> 00:49:35.784
Manny Azizi: what… what I take away from this is that the utilization of elastic energy doesn't have to be perfect, it doesn't have to be discrete, where you… where you describe a system either as utilizing spring actuation or utilizing muscle… direct muscle actuation, and that those two things


278
00:49:35.785 --> 00:49:37.445
Manny Azizi: can coexist.


279
00:49:37.745 --> 00:49:50.015
Manny Azizi: And to me, that really broadens the realm of the utilization of elastic energy, because it provides the opportunity to use it alongside muscle-driven behavior.


280
00:49:51.885 --> 00:49:52.815
Manny Azizi: Okay.


