00:51:51.145 --> 00:51:57.995
Manny Azizi: Yeah, so the latch here is the same as the latch in the frog, which is that we're starting the….


302
00:51:57.995 --> 00:52:06.945
Jill McNitt-Gray: We're starting our model in a highly crouched posture that is, effectively operating with a really low mechanical advantage.


303
00:52:06.945 --> 00:52:16.674
Manny Azizi: And then the mass has sort of an inertial constraint, that's kind of that dotted line, that you have to overcome that inertia in order for the mass to start to accelerate.


304
00:52:16.885 --> 00:52:20.494
Jill McNitt-Gray: Where I'm going with my question is, I'm thinking about


305
00:52:21.565 --> 00:52:27.024
Jill McNitt-Gray: Skilled jumpers in technical situations where they can manipulate that.


306
00:52:27.025 --> 00:52:27.495
Manny Azizi: Yes.


307
00:52:27.495 --> 00:52:30.584
Jill McNitt-Gray: Yeah, so this is very, very interesting.


308
00:52:31.285 --> 00:52:39.825
Jill McNitt-Gray: Yeah. The most interesting thing we've done with this system, is to jump these animals off of compliance substrates.


