00:54:04.175 --> 00:54:05.805
Jill McNitt-Gray: Interesting. And…


321
00:54:05.805 --> 00:54:13.495
Jill McNitt-Gray: And there's also, like, just so in the diver part, too, is there's some strategy of leaving some force generation capabilities


322
00:54:13.495 --> 00:54:34.104
Jill McNitt-Gray: during the recall. So you get max board bend on the way down, but the rotation happens on the way up. And so our model simulations were able to show that, right? So, but it's, like, strategically determining how to regulate the latch and reorienting the force. So it's very… this is really wonderful, so keep going. Thank you.


323
00:54:34.105 --> 00:54:35.025
Manny Azizi: Okay.


324
00:54:35.285 --> 00:54:48.344
Manny Azizi: So, I could talk about power amplification for hours, and I did just recently give, like, a two and a half hour talk on this topic, over a long period. So.


325
00:54:48.405 --> 00:55:01.834
Manny Azizi: Lots of other systems we could talk about, and I will say my systems, which were the frogs, were probably the least interesting of them all, compared to these amazing invertebrate systems and the capacities that they have, …


326
00:55:02.725 --> 00:55:03.585
Manny Azizi: Kisa.


327
00:55:05.765 --> 00:55:14.654
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: So, Manny, I just have a question here, and that is, like, I don't know whether you know this pretty cool paper from…


328
00:55:14.715 --> 00:55:25.855
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Ulit Farahat and Hugh Heard was published in PLOS back in, like, 2010. There's a two-muscle system. Yeah, I think it also relates to Jill's


329
00:55:26.935 --> 00:55:44.684
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: area. So the idea is that, you know, what they found is that a pair of antagonistic muscles operating together that were impedance matched to the load, which seems to really fit into your paradigm.


330
00:55:44.705 --> 00:55:55.294
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Could produce 7 times as much mechanical power, 7 times as much mechanical power as a single muscle.


331
00:55:55.635 --> 00:55:59.255
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: So, I think the question is, like.


332
00:55:59.645 --> 00:56:02.814
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Can you start to incorporate, like…


333
00:56:03.105 --> 00:56:11.544
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: You know, antagonistic muscles acting at a single joint into this kind of analysis.


334
00:56:12.055 --> 00:56:17.734
Manny Azizi: Yeah, you know, initially, when we were working on these, these topics.


335
00:56:18.035 --> 00:56:20.415
Manny Azizi: I was of the opinion that


336
00:56:20.795 --> 00:56:27.585
Manny Azizi: Muscle relaxation rates are simply too slow, to really be any good as a latch.


337
00:56:28.225 --> 00:56:42.355
Manny Azizi: I've come around on that to some degree, mainly because my view of systems that utilize elastic energy storage is great… has been greatly broadened. And to me.


338
00:56:42.355 --> 00:56:57.964
Manny Azizi: the idea of chasing the fastest and the most perfect system, I think has actually hindered our understanding of these systems, because we've focused on these, sort of, these high-performance, isolated examples.


339
00:56:58.125 --> 00:57:00.015
Manny Azizi: Which…


340
00:57:00.055 --> 00:57:11.094
Manny Azizi: Which effectively made me think, no, you know, even the fastest fiber type, that relaxation rate simply takes too long for the force to get out of the way.


341
00:57:11.095 --> 00:57:20.474
Manny Azizi: if an antagonist is holding it. But I'm of the opinion now that there are scenarios where bad latches are okay, where


342
00:57:20.895 --> 00:57:36.784
Manny Azizi: In fact, the compliance substrate is a great example where messing up the latch actually was beneficial to the overall performance of the system. So, I'm much less constrained in my thinking about what can and can't be a latch.


343
00:57:36.785 --> 00:57:42.985
Manny Azizi: And I have come around to the idea that you can use an antagonist as a latch


344
00:57:42.985 --> 00:58:04.135
Manny Azizi: Particularly if that antagonist not only is deactivating, but that you have a… the capacity, or you have other structural features that allow the moment arm of that muscle to also change rapidly during deactivation. Now you're really amplifying that drop in force, which is what you need to have a good latch.


345
00:58:04.395 --> 00:58:21.864
Manny Azizi: You need that force to get out of the way. Like, you need it to be high enough to hold the system, but to get out of the way as fast as possible. But in… yeah, so I'm starting to come around to the idea that you could play around with muscle moment arms,


346
00:58:22.165 --> 00:58:26.985
Manny Azizi: And make that deactivation fast enough to act as a latch.


347
00:58:27.665 --> 00:58:28.335
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Yes.


348
00:58:28.335 --> 00:58:29.595
Jill McNitt-Gray: Girls… go ahead.


349
00:58:29.885 --> 00:58:31.094
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: After you, Jill.


350
00:58:31.095 --> 00:58:50.735
Jill McNitt-Gray: I was going to say, going back to the two-joint muscle parts of this, see, the two-joint muscles are really interesting, because it controls, like, 50% of the mass above the hip, if you will. So, you can get kind of a two-for-one here in controlling center mass position, as well as the inertial aspects of it, like to hold it.


351
00:58:51.185 --> 00:59:08.604
Jill McNitt-Gray: get, you know, this again is that resistance part, but this is, like, why… going back to, I think, where you're going, Keith says, looking at the system, right? The combination of multi-joint control and how that effectively regulates these things that you've been able to simplify in your model, which is very exciting, right? So…


352
00:59:08.605 --> 00:59:13.785
Jill McNitt-Gray: That was all it's going to say, because I think there's something about the system dynamics that we want to bring back in.


353
00:59:13.885 --> 00:59:18.525
Jill McNitt-Gray: For these latching mechanisms, as well as the team of muscles that are involved.


354
00:59:19.295 --> 00:59:27.215
Manny Azizi: I agree, and I think, in fact, I really feel like this aspect of the field has been hindered by


355
00:59:27.405 --> 00:59:37.795
Manny Azizi: Chasing the fastest, the highest accelerations, like, the most impressive systems, which are fascinating and amazing, but…


356
00:59:38.615 --> 00:59:52.515
Manny Azizi: But they are divorced from some of the constraints that lots of other systems deal with, and I think of this as being ubiquitous. If you have a tendinous structure in series with the muscle, you simply can't get around it.


357
00:59:52.685 --> 01:00:02.204
Manny Azizi: When the muscle produces force, that will… the tendon will stretch, whether you want it to or not, like, there's no way around it. And so….


358
01:00:02.535 --> 01:00:14.604
Manny Azizi: There's lots of interesting complexity that I think we can learn from studying slower, less impressive systems where some cycling of energy is still going through the tendon.


359
01:00:15.124 --> 01:00:35.075
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Yeah, and my final point was just going to be that when muscles are changing length, we tend to think of, like, there's a canonical EC coupling delay. There's a canonical, you know, rate of muscle relaxation, but those are measured under isometric conditions, and as soon as you let the system move.


360
01:00:35.275 --> 01:00:47.594
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Those pretty much go out the window, and so, like, the length dependence of the rate of force development and the length dependence of the rate of force decay


361
01:00:47.704 --> 01:00:55.895
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Could substantially, you know, sort of, you know, obviate some of the, you know.


362
01:00:56.525 --> 01:01:01.295
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: Thinking about really long delays and muscles being Bad latches.


363
01:01:01.485 --> 01:01:02.034
Manny Azizi: Yep.


364
01:01:03.385 --> 01:01:14.945
Manny Azizi: Great. Okay, last but not least, I want to talk about power attenuation. I think this is actually the… an area that really needs our attention.


365
01:01:15.145 --> 01:01:24.004
Manny Azizi: there's only been a handful of folks who focused on this aspect of tendon function. I think, I think it's really cool and has some broad implications.


366
01:01:24.105 --> 01:01:25.225
Manny Azizi: So.


367
01:01:25.425 --> 01:01:34.895
Manny Azizi: Here's a couple of experiments we did, really simple stuff, like, nothing… nothing hard. Like, this is as simple as it gets. We have…


368
01:01:34.994 --> 01:01:46.095
Manny Azizi: an isolated muscle tendon unit, right there, where we're stimulating. We are driving it through this little stretch, and the gray is when we're stimulating it.


369
01:01:47.255 --> 01:01:58.945
Manny Azizi: Then, we just change the rate at which, or the velocity of that stretch that's applied to our muscle. So, really, we're just altering the velocity of that stretch.


370
01:01:58.955 --> 01:02:14.254
Manny Azizi: We're trying to actively stretch the muscle, but as you'll see, what we have is a pretty compliant spring in series with our muscle, and so as a result, it's actually pretty challenging to stretch the muscle fibers, okay? So…


371
01:02:14.745 --> 01:02:29.965
Manny Azizi: Here's a pattern that we get. So, we've got length up on top, we have force on the bottom panel, we have length of the muscle tendon unit up on top. That is the… that's effectively the length change we're imposing onto the muscle tendon unit.


372
01:02:30.565 --> 01:02:36.094
Manny Azizi: The dark black is where we get the highest velocity for the muscle tendon unit.


373
01:02:36.465 --> 01:02:48.915
Manny Azizi: What we actually see in terms of the behavior of the muscle fiber is that as we activate the muscle fiber, even though the muscle tendon unit is being stretched, our muscle fiber is shortening.


374
01:02:49.715 --> 01:02:53.344
Manny Azizi: It's shortening for much of the stretch phase.


375
01:02:53.695 --> 01:03:06.175
Manny Azizi: And then, the tendon is stretching our fascicle. So, in this first phase, the muscle is shortening, we're stretching the MTU, so we know the tendon is stretching.


376
01:03:06.955 --> 01:03:12.465
Manny Azizi: And once we get past peak force, the tendon is loading our fascicle.


377
01:03:13.095 --> 01:03:24.145
Manny Azizi: What's most interesting is look at the velocity of stretch applied to the MTU compared to the velocity of stretch applied to the fascicle by the tendon.


378
01:03:24.975 --> 01:03:42.225
Manny Azizi: the fascicle velocity is much lower, which is a good thing, because when we stretch our muscle fascicles at a high rate, they tend to get damaged. When we stretch those muscle fibers to really long lengths, they tend to get damaged. So here, what we're seeing is


379
01:03:42.325 --> 01:03:47.275
Manny Azizi: The muscle is shortening, so it's moving it to a safer spot.


380
01:03:47.615 --> 01:03:53.445
Manny Azizi: Then it's being stretched by the tendon at a lower velocity than we were stretching the MTU.


381
01:03:53.515 --> 01:04:13.244
Manny Azizi: So really, the tendon here is acting as a mechanical buffer to limit the power input to our fascicles. We're still dissipating the same amount of energy, we're just doing it in a much safer way by operating at shorter lengths, and by operating at lower stretch velocities.


382
01:04:13.875 --> 01:04:14.865
Manny Azizi: Plus.


383
01:04:14.995 --> 01:04:36.354
Manny Azizi: The tendon is a known entity to the muscle, whereas the environment is an unknown and unpredictable entity. So having this material in between our environment and our muscle allows us to load the system in a much more repeatable and dependable way.


384
01:04:37.805 --> 01:04:56.545
Manny Azizi: Here's the summary of that data, and this was work I did with Tom Roberts when I was a postdoc. We have fascicle velocity, and if the fascicle velocity was the same as the, as the MTU stretch we're applying to it, so let's say there's no tendon in place, you would simply follow that dotted line.


385
01:04:56.805 --> 01:04:57.635
Manny Azizi: Right?


386
01:04:57.795 --> 01:05:12.265
Manny Azizi: However, the fact that the tendon is slowing down the rate of stretch that's applied to our muscle fiber means that we're getting lower stretch velocities applied to our fascicle.


387
01:05:12.615 --> 01:05:16.635
Manny Azizi: Even as our rates of stretch start to get higher.


388
01:05:17.935 --> 01:05:18.865
Manny Azizi: Kisa?


389
01:05:21.835 --> 01:05:34.624
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: This is really cool, Manny. So, I'm just struggling to understand, is the gray bar activation, and is the activation coming on exactly at the beginning of the stretch?


390
01:05:34.625 --> 01:05:38.245
Manny Azizi: In these experiments, they came on right at the beginning of stretch, yeah.


391
01:05:38.425 --> 01:05:51.264
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: And how… have you looked at how the dynamics of the MTU fascicle interaction change if you change the timing of the activation?


392
01:05:51.265 --> 01:05:51.865
Manny Azizi: Yep.


393
01:05:52.295 --> 01:05:58.555
Manny Azizi: I had that in there, but I took that experiment out for, for time purposes.


394
01:05:58.905 --> 01:06:07.494
Manny Azizi: Long story short, Well, first of all, Pre-activation, anticipatory activation.


395
01:06:07.645 --> 01:06:15.435
Manny Azizi: Prior to an impact or an impending eccentric kind of behavior is very, very common.


396
01:06:15.555 --> 01:06:32.554
Manny Azizi: Like, it is the norm, and so we did want to look at, well, what are the benefits of turning that thing on earlier, prior to the stretch? So we did experiments where we did it 50 milliseconds before the stretch, right at the onset of stretch, 50 milliseconds after the onset of stretch.


397
01:06:32.755 --> 01:06:40.944
Manny Azizi: And what we found was actually the rates were… the maximum loading rates onto the fascicle were largely the same.


398
01:06:41.405 --> 01:06:46.625
Manny Azizi: What really differed between those was the operating lengths.


399
01:06:47.265 --> 01:06:59.315
Manny Azizi: by… if you start the… if you activate early, you get much more shortening. It moves you onto the ascending limb of the force-length curve before the stretch happens.


400
01:06:59.545 --> 01:07:22.574
Manny Azizi: So it kind of shoves everything onto safer and shorter lengths by going early, whereas the late activation didn't have that. There was less shortening, and then the stretch, and so you were sort of operating at maybe questionable lengths. And we would start those at, like, 1.25 of L0, for example, just to be at sort of


401
01:07:22.575 --> 01:07:27.685
Manny Azizi: slightly longer lanes. But yeah, it has a… it has an important effect on


402
01:07:27.685 --> 01:07:33.224
Manny Azizi: Where you're operating on the force-length relationship when you turn on… when you do the activation.


403
01:07:33.225 --> 01:07:39.154
Manny Azizi: There were a couple of other interesting pieces of data.


404
01:07:39.155 --> 01:07:55.385
Manny Azizi: total energy is the same, because we're imparting the same amount of energy into the system every time, and all we're doing is changing the timing. So it's just kind of like the shape of that fascicle length change trajectory is what differs.


405
01:07:56.015 --> 01:08:03.675
Jill McNitt-Gray: That's really important for risk. Indeed. Right? Yeah, so this is a nice part where it's not just energy, but risk aversion, right?


406
01:08:03.675 --> 01:08:04.055
Manny Azizi: Absolutely.


407
01:08:04.055 --> 01:08:09.474
Jill McNitt-Gray: And this is really important in this unsteady movement things that we're trying to all study.


408
01:08:09.475 --> 01:08:10.055
Manny Azizi: Indeed.


409
01:08:10.055 --> 01:08:11.035
Jill McNitt-Gray: Very cool.


410
01:08:12.225 --> 01:08:12.595
Manny Azizi: Okay.


411
01:08:12.595 --> 01:08:25.944
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: I also… maybe I could just say this, sorry. I mean, this is just a dumb comment, but maybe something that's worth looking into in the future is, like, from the stuff that I've done modeling, you know, Monica's guinea fowl and…


412
01:08:26.085 --> 01:08:41.315
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: And, James Wakeling and Nikolai Kona's rats, which are bipedal and quadrupedal. It seems like if you're quadrupedal, you have more, I mean, less pre-activation, so you can kind of save that…


413
01:08:42.015 --> 01:08:54.345
Kiisa Carla Nishikawa: So you have maybe a shorter total duration of activation, and you're saving the energy, because if you've got 4 legs, you just don't have the… you have more bandwidth for….


414
01:08:55.415 --> 01:09:12.784
Manny Azizi: spreading risk across four lists. Yeah, yeah, and to my knowledge, nobody's really looked at that, but I think it would be an interesting question. I mean, I'll show some in vivo data on turkeys that we have, but that's a bipedal system, so…


415
01:09:13.055 --> 01:09:14.535
Manny Azizi: they pre-activate.


416
01:09:15.435 --> 01:09:20.664
Manny Azizi: Okay, last experiment I want to talk about, same exact thing.


417
01:09:20.915 --> 01:09:28.185
Manny Azizi: The only difference we did was we changed the temperature again, mainly because what we found was that


418
01:09:28.695 --> 01:09:35.574
Manny Azizi: Let me go back, actually. What we found was… The rate of, …


419
01:09:36.265 --> 01:09:43.444
Manny Azizi: This rate here really depends on the rate of relaxation in the muscle.


420
01:09:44.115 --> 01:09:48.744
Manny Azizi: Because… That's really what's determining the…


421
01:09:48.925 --> 01:09:51.944
Manny Azizi: Release of force from the tendon.


422
01:09:52.125 --> 01:09:59.275
Manny Azizi: So, if… the faster the muscle deactivates, the faster the tendon is going to load the muscle.


423
01:09:59.685 --> 01:10:06.005
Manny Azizi: So, deactivation rate ended up being really a critical parameter for determining that maximum velocity.


424
01:10:06.695 --> 01:10:07.315
Jill McNitt-Gray: Hmm.


425
01:10:08.075 --> 01:10:19.155
Manny Azizi: And so, we decided to try to shift it by doing these temperature perturbations, where we can increase the deactivation rate as the system gets hotter.


426
01:10:19.405 --> 01:10:24.555
Manny Azizi: So, here are the patterns we see at 10 degrees and at 30 degrees.


427
01:10:24.835 --> 01:10:35.375
Manny Azizi: What you'll notice is at 30 degrees, because the muscle can develop force much more rapidly, we get this initial shortening right there.


428
01:10:35.575 --> 01:10:38.724
Manny Azizi: Before the tendon stretches the muscle.


429
01:10:39.265 --> 01:10:45.965
Manny Azizi: Whereas at 10 degrees, because the muscle is being kind of slow, we're not seeing that initial shortening happen.


430
01:10:46.375 --> 01:10:48.795
Manny Azizi: And so, in fact, …


431
01:10:49.755 --> 01:10:55.345
Manny Azizi: That early shift in the position of where the muscle's operating, that's no longer there.


432
01:10:55.985 --> 01:11:00.815
Manny Azizi: And then, more importantly, as we change our…


433
01:11:00.995 --> 01:11:08.015
Manny Azizi: properties, what we see is that, okay, that fascicle shortening is much less at 10 degrees early on.


434
01:11:08.095 --> 01:11:26.295
Manny Azizi: But then later on, we find that the fascicle lengthening is a little bit more when we have it at higher, at, higher temperatures, and the rate at which it's being loaded is significantly higher as well. So, ….


